## Chapter 1.2: The Man on the News

Luka piece together what he had seen in his vision, remembering as much as he can, he slowly let the vision faded. Once Luka’s eyes started darting around again, Nadezhda prompted,

“You’re back? I got a bit scared there. You’ve been spacing out for…” she checked the time on her terminal, “3 minutes…”

“—Oh, yeah, I’m back,” Luka shook his head left and right, clearing off his blurry sight.

“So… what did you see. What will be my future?” She leaned in, eagerly waiting for an answer.

“W-Well, Comrade Officer—” Luka staggered. He learned a lot of details in the vision. He should reflect his statement, “Um, no. I mean, excuse me if I get this wrong but… Comrade Morozova, in my vision you will be promoted to the Senior Lieutenant rank this year.”

Nadezhda eyes widen in surprise when she heard her name uttered from his mouth. This was their first time meeting, and there was no other way that he could know her name. Except…

She let out a giggle, “Aha! You almost got me! And I thought ‘how would you know my name!?’ but then I remembered,” she picked up her name plate sitting right in front of Luka all these time. The irony was that he hadn’t notice it until now. “And look, me getting promoted this year? And skipping a rank as well? I’m flattered, comrade, but you don’t have to make up story to make me feel good,” She smiled gently, believing that it was too good to be true.

But then Luka just let out a big sigh. She would have believed him if her name plate wasn’t there. He didn’t even know what was the reason for her promotion, so he couldn’t pursue it. There was nothing left to convince her.

Seeing his disappointed and despair face, she realized that she should take him seriously. She dropped her smile and re-approach him calmly, “Hey, I was speaking as a Millitsiya earlier, but let me be real with you for a moment,” she spoke sincerely, “I know you are very serious about this, comrade. I can’t promise you much, but if it will make you more at ease, I can record your report in the database,” she offered a compromise. 

Luka eyes lights up with a glimmer of hope once he heard that. It wasn’t an immediate solution like he had wished but being recognized, even a little, was a big relief for him.

“Oh, yes! Please do, Comrade Morozova! Thank you!” Luka bowed his head in gratitude out of habit.

“Don’t mention it, happy to help,” she reassured. “Alright, now, is your name spelled exactly like on your badge, Comrade Kho…?” realizing that his last name was quite strange, she asked, “Are you Chinese?”

“Yes, half-Chinese, my dad is Chinese and my mom is Russian,” Luka replied.

“I see, so it’s pronounced, Huo, yes?” she tried to pronounce it with an H sounds, not completely accurate but better.

“Yes, but Kho is fine too, I know it’s a bit hard for Russian speaker,” Luka sympathize, in fact, there are many Russian consonants that he had a tough time pronouncing it too.

“No, no, it’s better that I try. Your Russian is pretty much native; pronouncing your name correctly is the least I can do,” she humbly replied. 

“Alright, now, who is your best friend, Comrade Huo?” she asked.

“Aleksey Volkov, hardware engineer at PRIM,” he answered.

She typed in the his name in the terminal, after a few seconds, she sync the data with an e-paper she had in had on hand. She slid it to Luka for review, the pigment on the e-paper gradually set into a crisp high-definition portrait in half a second.

“Is this Aleksey Volkov?” She asked.

“Yes, this is him, no doubt,” He replied. That sharp, handsome face, with a slick well-groomed brown hair. The classic popular guy type. Despite looking like a total jerk, he was actually pretty nice and kind and smart. Luka often wondering why they became best friend in the first place.

Once she heard his confirmation, she clicked on her terminal to sync the e-paper to a report form, “Alright, please fill in the form, here, here, and here,” she pointed out and handing him a stylus. During the paperwork, she inquired more about his vision of Aleksey while simultaneously noting it down in her terminal. The info sync with the e-paper, as the character she typed pop up with a little delay on the sheet.

Luka finished filling out the form but noticed that Nadezhda glancing back up and down from her terminal to the regular sheet of paper, copying what she wrote using the classic pen and paper. Noticing Luka’s gaze, she said, “Please excuse me, the new post-Ripple protocol calls for ‘non-electronic’ copy of all documents…”

Luka nodded, he was also going through the same protocol at PIRM.

After a bit, she handed her the paper and pen to let him fill out the rest. After he was done, she compared both electronic and non-electronic version before putting both into her drawer. 

“Alright, Comrade Huo, your report had been recorded in the system. If there is any further development, please let us know,” she smiled warmly.

“Thank you, Comrade Morozova. It truly means a lot to me,” Although he still worried about Alex, at least he didn’t keep it to himself and told someone.

“Gladly,” she replied but before Luka got up, she spoke up, “Before you go, Comrade. Have you seen this person yesterday, or last night?” She slid another e-paper to Luka, this time it’s a wanted poster of Igor Romashin. A familiar name, Luka was sure that he had seen this man before on the news…

